      "costUsd": 0.008165,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. Forty metres of fencing make three sides of a sheep pen, and an old stone wall makes the fourth. Seen from above, let's try some shapes. Five metres out from the wall leaves thirty metres along it, so the area is a hundred and fifty square metres. Fifteen out leaves ten along, a hundred and fifty again. A square pen, thirteen and a third metres each way, gives about a hundred and seventy-eight. But ten out and twenty along gives two hundred. So the square isn't the best shape here. How do we find the best one, and be sure nothing beats it?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-d18/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1145,
