      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 1023,
      "costUsd": 0.0092785,
      "pronunciationText": "Which values of x make a real pen? x has to be positive, or there's no width. And y has to be positive too. Forty minus two x is positive only when x is less than twenty. So the domain is x between zero and twenty, with neither end included. At each end the pen collapses. Near zero it's a thin strip along the wall, and near twenty it's squashed flat against it. The area gets close to zero at both ends, but those aren't pens, so zero is a limit, not a value the area reaches. Stating the domain matters, because it decides which stationary points count, and what we compare at the ends."
    },
